From 6b77e9c272f62b6d7a870d22b631e7aca516f0a9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nathan Singh <nathan.singh@hotmail.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 06:40:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Native: a wider screen (16:10 and 16:9), first slice

The desktop build picks its screen width at start-up: --width 240, 256
or 288 (3:2, 16:10, about 16:9), or TBS_WIDTH. The GBA build is
unchanged (byte-identical ROM).

- DISPLAY_WIDTH is the variable gNativeDisplayWidth in the native build
  (io_reg.h), so code that already goes by it follows. The renderer's
  buffers are sized for the widest screen and draw the chosen width.
- The field's three layers draw into 64-tile-wide maps: a text background
  whose control register has bit 13 set (unused by text backgrounds on the
  GBA) reads a native wide map instead of VRAM (native/wide_map.h).
  field_map_grid.c takes its map width, stride and screen from macros
  that keep the GBA's values.
- The camera's focus and bounds use the screen's width natively.
- Window registers hold 8-bit edges, so WIN_RANGE(0, 288) had wrapped to
  32 pixels and hid the battle backdrop past x=32. Natively WIN_RANGE
  writes a right edge past 255 as 240, and the display takes a right edge
  of 240 or more as the screen's edge.

Not yet: the window layer (menus, messages, battle HUD) is 32 tiles wide;
at 288 its boxes wrap and the battle command row crashes.

